title: Editing forked content in a view
pid: 48c3f1f7-0b1e-4c1b-8fea-1980138dfe37
body: |2-
  The viewchildren of a forkee as such can be edited (in limited ways) to control what its forker hides from it.

  Let's call the forker R and the forkee E: R forks E. In a view where R is editable, E stands in R's forks folder drawn editable, as content is (unless another occurrence of E in the buffer is editable, or the node budget is spent), showing its content less what R contains or hides, and less what another note of yours contains.

  If N is graph-content of E, and you delete N as a viewchild of E, you have indicated to Skg that you would like to hide N from R's view of E. Skg obliges by creating a hides relationship from R to N, unless R, or another note of yours, contains N after the save: then you keep N there, and no hide is needed. This does /not/ change what E graph-contains; it just changes how R views E. (Remember, it's likely that E is not even something you own.)

  If you later put that same child back among E's children in R's forks folder and save, the hide is removed.

  This rule only applies to the forkee's children. Edits to deeper descendants have the usual effect, subject to ownership.

  Only an R you own records hides: where R is foreign, a save that would hide or unhide anything in R is refused, naming R.

  If you type a new headline under E, or move there a node E does not contain, it joins E's content, right after the child it follows, when you own E; under a foreign E the save is refused, since only E's owner can change its content (make E your own first, from a view of E as itself, where an edit starts the fork operation).

  Title and body edits to E made there are saved when you own E. A foreign E's are refused: make them from some view that does not present E as a forkee as such (C-c g RET from E will open such a view), where an edit starts the fork operation.

  Two generated folders show what R currently hides. (A third, first under E, shows what E contains that you keep elsewhere; it records nothing, and a child R hides stands in the hidden-in folder alone.) Under E, the hidden-in folder (=hiddenInForkPartnerFolder=, "☮ It contains these, but c hides them.") lists what R hides that E contains; it is a write-protected display, and editing it is not how you hide or unhide forked content. To hide something, remove it from E's immediate visible graph-content in R's forks folder, keeping it in none of your notes. To unhide it, put it back there. Last in R's forks folder, the hidden-outside folder (=hiddenOutsideOfForkPartnersFolder=, "b hides these, but no fork partner shown contains them.") lists what R hides that no member shown in that folder contains (no forkee, no forker R does not hide, and no indirect member R does not hide), a forker R hides among them; it is editable, and removing a member there unhides it (=glossary.org=, "compound partner folder").
